The star chart has its own Escape handler (`onClose`). The star chart's e2e presses Escape to close the chart — if the chart is opened by the M key (standalone), in the new design M pushes 'chart' onto the menu stack. But the chart's own Escape handler calls onClose → setChartOpen(false) — and the global stack handler also pops 'chart'. Both run on the same keydown event: the chart's handler (registered on window) + the global handler (window). Order: whichever was added first. Double action: the chart handler sets chartOpen to false (doesn't pop the stack) → the stack still has 'chart'; the global handler pops 'chart' (no other side effect). Net: chart closes + stack pops. Actually that's consistent! But there's an ordering risk: if the global handler runs first (pops 'chart' → setChartOpen(false)) and then the chart's handler runs (onClose → setChartOpen(false) again, idempotent). Either way, no problem.

But there's an edge case: when the menu is open, chart is open (stack: [menu, chart]). ESC → the chart's own handler closes the chart (setChartOpen false) + the global pops 'chart' → stack [menu]. Good. Then ESC again: the chart's handler is unmounted (chartOpen false) → the global pops 'menu' → closes.

And when the chart is standalone (stack: [chart]): ESC → both handlers → chart closes + pops. Then ESC → stack empty → opens the menu! Wait — the e2e star-chart spec: press Escape to close the chart; does it then press Escape again? Let me check: the spec says "closes on Escape". If it presses Escape twice, the second press will now open the menu — probably harmless for the test (asserts that the chart is hidden). But for UX: pressing ESC after closing the chart opens the menu — that's actually the intended behavior (ESC toggles the menu). Hmm, but within the same key event... no, separate presses.

Wait, actually there's a subtle bug risk: the chart's own ESC handler fires onClose unconditionally on Escape (even while typing in the search input! The search input is inside the chart — typing ESC in the search box closes the chart. This is existing behavior... existing: `if (e.key === 'Escape') onClose();` — no input check. OK, existing behavior; keep it, but the global handler has an input check. When typing in the chart's search box: the chart's handler closes the chart; the global handler sees the input target → ignores (doesn't pop the stack!). Mismatch: chartOpen false but the stack still has 'chart' → input is suppressed + the next ESC pops the ghost. To avoid this mess: remove the chart's own Escape handler and let the global stack handler own all ESC. Then typing ESC in the search input → the global ignores (input target) → the chart stays open. This is a behavior change (previously ESC while searching closed the chart) — acceptable and more correct.

So: remove the chart's own Esc effect. The global handler in App:
